# gstack for Windsurf (Codeium Cascade)

> gstack is Garry Tan's open-source "software factory" — 23 specialist roles
> (CEO reviewer, eng manager, designer, QA lead, release engineer, security
> officer, …) plus power tools, all as Markdown skills. This file wires those
> roles into Windsurf's Cascade agent.

## Setup (one time)

```bash
# requires: git, Bun (https://bun.sh), Node.js
git clone --single-branch --depth 1 https://github.com/garrytan/gstack.git ~/.windsurf/skills/gstack
cd ~/.windsurf/skills/gstack && ./setup --host windsurf
```

This installs gstack's Windsurf skill docs into `~/.windsurf/skills/` and
symlinks the runtime assets (`bin/`, `browse/dist/`, `review/`, …) into
`~/.windsurf/skills/gstack/` so generated skill preambles can resolve
`$GSTACK_ROOT`, `$GSTACK_BIN`, `$GSTACK_BROWSE`.

To get the skill docs committed into **this** repo for your whole team
(recommended):

```bash
~/.windsurf/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-team-init required
git add .windsurf/ AGENTS.md && git commit -m "require gstack for AI-assisted work"
```

## How to use gstack from Cascade

Talk to Cascade naturally and ask it to load a gstack role. Each role is a
self-contained instruction file under `~/.windsurf/skills/gstack-<role>/SKILL.md`
(or `.windsurf/skills/gstack-<role>/SKILL.md` in this repo).

| You say | gstack role |
|---------|-------------|
| "Plan this feature with gstack" | load `gstack-autoplan` (CEO → design → eng → DX review) |
| "Office-hours on this idea" | load `gstack-office-hours` |
| "Review this branch with gstack" | load `gstack-review` |
| "QA the staging URL" | load `gstack-qa` (opens a real browser) |
| "Ship this branch" | load `gstack-ship` (test → review → push → PR) |
| "Security audit this repo" | load `gstack-cso` (OWASP Top 10 + STRIDE) |
| "Investigate this bug" | load `gstack-investigate` |
| "Lock edits to src/" | load `gstack-freeze` / `gstack-guard` |

Full role catalog: see `AGENTS.md` (cross-agent) or run
`~/.windsurf/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-config` after install.

## Workflow that works in Windsurf

1. **Plan before code.** `gstack-office-hours` → `gstack-autoplan`. Save the
   plan; don't implement yet.
2. **Implement.** Write the code. Use `gstack-careful`/`gstack-freeze` to scope
   edits when the task must not touch other directories.
3. **Review.** `gstack-review` finds bugs that pass CI but break in production.
4. **QA.** `gstack-qa` drives a real Chromium browser against your staging URL.
5. **Ship.** `gstack-ship` runs tests, opens a PR; `gstack-land-and-deploy`
   merges, waits for CI + deploy, verifies prod health.

## Conventions Cascade must follow

- gstack skills are **generated** Markdown — read them, don't edit them. Edit
  the `.tmpl` sources and run `bun run gen:skill-docs --host windsurf`.
- The browse binary provides headless browser access. Inside a skill, use
  `$B <command>` (the preamble exports `$B`).
- State paths resolve via `bin/gstack-paths` (honor `GSTACK_HOME`).
- Safety skills (`careful`, `freeze`, `guard`) are advisory — **always confirm
  with the user before destructive operations** (rm -rf, DROP TABLE, force-push).
- Stay focused on repository code. Do NOT read or execute skill definitions
  installed for other AI systems under `~/.claude/`, `~/.codex/`, `~/.kiro/`,
  `.agents/`, `.claude/skills/` — they are not meant for Windsurf and will waste
  your time. Your gstack docs live under `~/.windsurf/skills/` and
  `.windsurf/skills/`.

## Upgrade

```bash
~/.windsurf/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-upgrade   # or re-run ./setup --host windsurf
```
